<article class="article-body">

<p>Year-end planning tends to produce the same kind of org chart: a list of titles the company doesn't have yet, mapped against a budget that can't fund all of them. What usually gets missed is a step earlier than "who do we hire": which of these gaps are structural, requiring a permanent functional owner, and which are actually a founder coordination problem wearing a job title as a disguise.</p>

<h2>Start with where the founder's time actually went this year</h2>
<p>Before planning next year's hires, look honestly at where this year's hours went. If a meaningful share went to things that weren't product, sales, or fundraising, calendar triage, cross-team coordination, chasing decisions that stalled, that's a data point most org-chart exercises skip entirely. A gap in the founder's calendar is just as real as a gap in the headcount plan, and it often doesn't show up until someone looks for it directly.</p>

<h2>Not every 2027 gap needs a permanent hire</h2>
<p>Some gaps are genuinely structural: a growing team needs a permanent head of sales, a scaling product needs a dedicated engineering lead. Others are temporary by nature, a market entry that needs coordinating this year, a fundraise that needs running point, an operations rebuild that needs doing once and then handed off. Treating a temporary need as a permanent hire is one of the more expensive planning mistakes a growing company makes.</p>

<h2>Where a chief of staff fits in a 2027 plan</h2>
<p>A chief of staff is well suited to exactly the gaps that don't fit neatly on a permanent org chart: absorbing coordination load across functions while the company figures out what its real structural needs are, running a defined project like a market entry or fundraise, or building the operating systems a future functional hire will eventually inherit. It's a way to buy a year of clarity before committing to permanent, expensive headcount decisions.</p>

<h2>A practical way to plan it</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>List this year's recurring founder time-sinks</strong>, not just the roles that feel obviously missing.</li>
  <li><strong>Separate structural gaps from temporary ones</strong>: a permanent function versus a defined project or a transitional need.</li>
  <li><strong>Budget fractional or flexible support for the temporary gaps</strong>, rather than defaulting every need to a full-time hire.</li>
  <li><strong>Revisit the plan at the ninety-day mark</strong>, not just at next year-end, since what looked structural in December can look different by March.</li>
</ul>

